package agent

const sendWidgetDescription = `Create and send a custom Telegram widget in the current private chat.

For kind=html, generate original, self-contained HTML with inline CSS and JavaScript for the user's request. You are not limited to predefined templates: make charts, comparison cards, summaries, tabs, filters, or small local interactive tools. Put the actual HTML in html; do not just describe it or send its source as a text reply.

Visual quality is part of the task. Include purposeful inline CSS and deliver a polished miniature interface, even when the user has not separately asked for styling. Choose a visual direction suited to the content: clear typography and hierarchy, a consistent spacing rhythm, aligned content, a restrained coherent palette, and thoughtfully styled controls with visible selected/pressed/disabled states. Give important metrics prominence; use charts, progress bars or inline SVG when they communicate actual data. Avoid an unstyled document with default headings, lists and browser buttons, and avoid decorative clutter or invented chart data. Do not force every widget into the same generic card layout.

Use system-ui fonts and Telegram's --tg-theme-* CSS variables with useful fallbacks so light and dark themes remain readable. Fit a narrow message bubble, let long labels wrap, keep tap targets comfortable, and make empty/loading/error states look intentional. The widget is capped at 600 pixels high and has no internal scrolling: summarize or paginate longer lists locally so content is not clipped. Before sending, check contrast, hierarchy, spacing, label overflow and whether the controls clearly explain what they do. Stay within the byte budget by simplifying the design, not by dropping all styling.

Inline SVG and canvas are available. Only buttons are supported as form controls: no input, select, textarea, keyboard focus, or keyboard events. No external scripts, libraries, fonts, images, or network requests. Use local JavaScript variables for tabs and filters. For data that Albert can update later, pass JSON as chat_data or public_data, read TelegramWidget.chatData/publicData, redraw on chatDataChanged/publicDataChanged with TelegramWidget.onEvent, and call TelegramWidget.ready() after drawing. Render data with textContent instead of inserting it as HTML. Custom HTML uses allow_queries=false; its local JavaScript still runs and can react to taps.

Include a meaningful plain-text caption summarizing the useful content for older clients and accessibility. Limits: html 1–32768 UTF-8 bytes and at most 4096 bytes after zlib-9; caption at most 1024 UTF-16 units; each state string at most 1024 UTF-8 bytes. When fallback=false, use edit_widget_data with the returned message_id to change server data later. With fallback=true, that ID belongs to a text message, so provide subsequent updates as text instead. HTML is immutable: send a new widget when its code needs changing.

For kind=schedules, provide 1–4 task_ids from task_find for the built-in deterministic pause/resume panel, including inline buttons and keyboard commands. Only this registered template supports allow_queries and task actions.

Returns the sent message_id and whether text fallback was used. Success delivers a real reply and does not end the turn. Finish naturally when complete; do not duplicate the widget with a separate text confirmation. Never use for intermediate progress.`

const customWidgetRules = `
When send_widget is listed, you can create custom Telegram widgets on the fly by generating HTML, inline CSS and JavaScript with kind=html. Fulfil an explicit widget request with this tool, rather than sending a code block, describing a possible widget, or limiting the user to the schedules template. You may also choose a widget when an interactive chart, comparison or compact dashboard materially helps. Keep ordinary conversation as text. A custom widget must look like a finished, thoughtfully designed interface: include intentional CSS, clear visual hierarchy, good typography, spacing, a coherent palette and polished controls. Do not send bare browser-default HTML unless the user explicitly asks for a plain version. Adapt the design to its content, narrow bubbles and light/dark themes; follow the tool's visual-quality guidance and sandbox limits. Custom widgets can have local tabs, filters, buttons and charts. Include a useful caption as a text alternative. A successful send_widget is a real delivered reply, including when its text fallback was used: do not send another message just to confirm it. When fallback=false, use edit_widget_data to refresh a custom widget's data using its returned message_id; fallback=true identifies a text reply that cannot be edited as a widget. Only listed tools and registered actions can change server state; generated JavaScript handles local interaction.
`
